When flexbox "doesn't work," it's almost always because the **parent container isn't tall enough** to push things around in. By default, a `div` only takes up as much height as its content. 

Here is the complete, foolproof way to do it.

### 1. Make sure your HTML looks something like this:
```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child">I am centered!</div>
</div>
```

### 2. Use this exact CSS:
```css
/* 1. Make the parent take up the full screen height */
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Centers horizontally */
  align-items: center;     /* Centers vertically */

  height: 100vh;           /* Crucial: 100% of the viewport height */
  margin: 0;               /* Remove default body/container margins if needed */
}
```

---

### Still not working? Check these 3 common culprits:

1. **Are you applying it to the `<body>`?** 
   If you want to center something in the exact middle of the whole browser window, the `<body>` and `<html>` tags also need to have a height of `100%`.
   ```css
   html, body {
     height: 100%;
     margin: 0;
   }
   ```
2. **Is there another parent in the way?** 
   If your `.parent` div is nested inside another `div` that is squished to the top, `.parent` will just center itself inside that squished space. 
3. **Are you trying to center it relative to the viewport instead?**
   If you just want the `div` dead-center on the screen regardless of HTML structure, apply the flexbox directly to the `<body>`:
   ```css
   body {
     display: flex;
     justify-content: center;
     align-items: center;
     height: 100vh;
     margin: 0;
   }
   ```

